@node DFFI Reference
@subsection DFFI Reference
@cindex Dynamic foreign function interface

@lspindex ffi:*use-dffi*
@defvr Variable {ffi:*use-dffi*}
This variable controls whether DFFI is used or not.
@end defvr


@subsubsection Structures by value

The dynamic interface passes and returns structures by value. A
structure argument is given as foreign data (@pxref{Foreign objects})
holding at least as many bytes as the structure occupies; a structure
result comes back as fresh foreign data of the structure's size, tagged
with its type, and is read with @code{ffi:get-slot-value} like any
other.

@code{ffi:def-function} and @code{ffi:defcallback} resolve the type
names themselves. @code{si:call-cfun}, the primitive beneath them, reads
its designators in C and cannot look a name up: hand it the
@code{(:struct (@var{name} @var{type}) ...)} list the name stands for,
which @code{ffi::%convert-to-ffi-type} returns. Inside a structure a
member may itself be a structure or an @code{(:array @var{type}
@var{n})}; a pointer member is a pointer whatever it points to.

Unions are refused. libffi has no union type, and the usual imitation --
a structure holding only the largest member -- classifies wrongly for
register passing exactly when the members disagree about their class,
which is the case that matters.

@lisp
(ffi:def-struct range (location :long) (length :long))

(si:call-cfun (si:find-foreign-symbol "range_make" :default :pointer-void 0)
              (ffi::%convert-to-ffi-type 'range)
              '(:long :long)
              '(6 5))
@result{} #<foreign (:STRUCT (LOCATION :LONG) (LENGTH :LONG)) ...>
@end lisp
